  "exploiter_output": {
    "evidence": "Root cause / sink: `spark.resource.ExternalResourceHandler#getResource(String)` at src/main/java/spark/resource/ExternalResourceHandler.java:59-69, specifically the `new ExternalResource(addedPath)` call at line 69, which delegates to `ExternalResource(String path)` (src/main/java/spark/resource/ExternalResource.java:39-40) doing `file = new File(StringUtils.cleanPath(path))`. Trust boundary: `path`/`addedPath` derives from `HttpServletRequest#getServletPath()`/`getPathInfo()` (attacker-controlled HTTP request path) via `AbstractResourceHandler#getResource(HttpServletRequest)` (AbstractResourceHandler.java:46-59) \u2192 `addPaths()` (AbstractResourceHandler.java:58,80-121). The resolved `File`/URL is never checked against the configured base resource root before being read/streamed (StaticFilesConfiguration.java:86-92, AbstractFileResolvingResource.java:44-46/106-112).\n\nConfirmed mechanism: `UriPath.canonical(path)` (UriPath.java:32-34, logic at lines 30-181) only treats `/` as a path separator, so a request path containing Windows-style `..\\` sequences is returned completely unmodified (no traversal detected, nothing rejected). The unmodified string is concatenated onto the base resource root by `addPaths()` and only THEN normalized by `StringUtils.cleanPath()` (StringUtils.java:200-266), which does treat `\\` as a separator and does collapse `..` \u2014 but, unlike `UriPath.canonical()`, it never rejects a path whose leftover `..` count exceeds the number of consumable preceding segments (see the \"Remaining top paths need to be retained\" block, StringUtils.java run at ~255-263): those excess `..` are just prepended to the result. The resulting `File`/URL is used directly to check existence and stream content with no containment check anywhere downstream.\n\nEvery distinct sink/source-to-sink path in the alert sharing this exact root cause that a complete fix must guard:\n1. (POV'd here) `AbstractResourceHandler.getResource(HttpServletRequest)` \u2192 `ExternalResourceHandler.getResource(String)` \u2192 `new ExternalResource(addedPath)` \u2192 `ExternalResource.file` (File read) \u2014 ExternalResourceHandler.java:59-69, ExternalResource.java:39-40/77-78, verified experimentally: servletPath `\"/..\\secret.txt\"` against base `<tmp>/public` resolves to and reads `<tmp>/secret.txt`.\n2. `ExternalResourceHandler.getResource(String)` welcome-file branch: `new ExternalResource(addPaths(resource.getPath(), welcomeFile))` (ExternalResourceHandler.java:65-67) \u2014 same `ExternalResource` constructor sink, reachable when the traversed target resolves to a directory and a welcome file is configured.\n3. `AbstractResourceHandler.getResource(HttpServletRequest)` \u2192 `ClassPathResourceHandler.getResource(String)` \u2192 `new ClassPathResource(addedPath)` \u2192 `ClassPathResource.path` field, consumed by `getInputStream()`/`exists()` via `ClassLoader.getResource(this.path)` (ClassPathResourceHandler.java:60-70, ClassPathResource.java:61-81/136-139) \u2014 experimentally confirmed the identical backslash bypass (`\"/..\\cp-secret.txt\"`) escapes a classpath-exploded-directory base and reads a sibling file on the classpath root.\n4. `ClassPathResourceHandler.getResource(String)` welcome-file branch: `new ClassPathResource(addPaths(resource.getPath(), welcomeFile))` (ClassPathResourceHandler.java:66-70) and the `resource.getFile()` directory check (ClassPathResourceHandler.java:72, via `AbstractFileResolvingResource.getFile()` \u2192 `ResourceUtils.getFile(url,...)`) \u2014 same `ClassPathResource` sink family, also reachable via `AbstractResourceHandler#getServletPath()`-only branch (no INCLUDE attributes / no pathInfo), i.e. line 50 of AbstractResourceHandler.java, confirming the source is not limited to the `RequestDispatcher.INCLUDE_*` attribute branch.\n\nAll four converge on the same defect: `UriPath.canonical()` is a no-op for backslash-delimited traversal, and `StringUtils.cleanPath()` (the only later norm