    "expected_vulnerable_behavior": "On the current (unpatched) code, ExternalResourceHandler.getResource(request) resolves a non-null ExternalResource for the crafted servlet path \"/..\\secret.txt\" whose canonical file path is `<tempRoot>/secret.txt` \u2014 strictly outside the configured base resource directory `<tempRoot>/public` \u2014 and resource.getInputStream() streams back the exact contents of that out-of-root file (\"TOP-SECRET-CONTENT-1234\"). The JUnit test asserts exactly this and exits 0. A correct fix must make getResource(...) reject/return null (or otherwise fail the assertions) for this request, causing the same command to exit non-zero.",
    "notes": "Targeted alert trace: vulnerability #1 (and its sibling #4, which is the same ExternalResource/ExternalResourceHandler sink family reached via the welcome-file re-resolution branch) \u2014 i.e. AbstractResourceHandler.getResource(HttpServletRequest) \u2192 ExternalResourceHandler.getResource(String) \u2192 UriPath.canonical \u2192 addPaths \u2192 new ExternalResource(path) \u2192 StringUtils.cleanPath \u2192 new File(...) \u2192 AbstractFileResolvingResource.getFile(). Chosen as the primary PoV because: (1) it demonstrates the clearest, most severe consequence \u2014 arbitrary out-of-root file disclosure via the real public request-handling API, not a synthetic call into an internal method; (2) it is the simplest, fully deterministic reproduction (a single \"..\" via backslash cancels exactly one path segment, landing precisely on a sibling file regardless of the temp directory's absolute depth \u2014 no dependency on filesystem layout like /etc/passwd); (3) vulnerabilities #2/#3 (ClassPathResourceHandler/ClassPathResource) share the exact same root-cause defect (UriPath.canonical() blind to backslashes + StringUtils.cleanPath()'s no-reject leftover-\"..\" behavior) and were independently verified working with an identical payload against a classpath-exploded-directory base (target/classes), confirming a correct fix must cover both handler families, not just the one exercised by this PoV. That secondary confirmation was done via a throwaway exploratory harness (not retained) and is documented in `evidence`/`regression_hint` for the patcher.\n\nThe PoV file is self-contained (default package, no changes to pom.xml or src/test/java) and is compiled/run manually via javac + org.junit.runner.JUnitCore in the pov_command, which naturally exits 0 on success / 1 on JUnit failure. Verified both directions inside the project's docker container: (a) exit 0 against the pristine repo (git status clean save for the new pov files); (b) exit 1 after temporarily adding a canonical-path containment check to ExternalResourceHandler.getResource(String) to simulate a correct fix \u2014 that temporary edit was fully reverted afterward (git status confirmed clean again) before finalizing this report.",
