    "regression_hint": "Invariant a correct fix must enforce, at every sink above: after resolving a request path against a configured base resource root (external filesystem root for ExternalResourceHandler/ExternalResource, or classpath/base folder for ClassPathResourceHandler/ClassPathResource \u2014 including each handler's welcome-file re-resolution branch), the FINAL resolved absolute/canonical location (File.getCanonicalPath() for ExternalResource; the resolved URL/File for ClassPathResource) MUST start with the configured base root's canonical path (with a trailing separator, or an exact-prefix-plus-separator check, to avoid `/public-evil` bypassing a naive `startsWith(\"/public\")` check). If it does not, the handler must return null (treat as not-found), not silently serve/stream the escaped resource.\n\nThis must be enforced uniformly, not just by trying to patch `UriPath.canonical()` to also recognize `\\`, because:\n- `UriPath.canonical()` operates on the request path alone, before it is concatenated with the base resource root, so even a correct canonicalizer only proves the request path doesn't escape *itself* \u2014 it cannot prove the final concatenated path doesn't escape the *base root* (e.g. sequences that are \"legal\" relative to the request path's own leading `/` but still net-negative once combined with a short base root). The containment check must happen after concatenation/resolution, at (or immediately after) the point the `File`/`URL` is actually constructed.\n- `StringUtils.cleanPath()` must not be trusted as a security boundary either: it silently keeps leftover `..` instead of rejecting, by design (it's a generic path-normalization utility, not a sandboxing one).\n\nKnown bypass variants the fix must also block (beyond the base `/../` case that `UriPath.canonical()` already partially blocks):\n- Backslash-delimited traversal: `..\\`, `..\\\\`, `a\\..\\..\\etc\\passwd` (demonstrated in the PoV).\n- Mixed separators: `..\\/..\\/etc/passwd`, `.\\/../` combinations.\n- Traversal reachable via the welcome-file re-resolution path (`ExternalResourceHandler`/`ClassPathResourceHandler` lines ~65-70), where a second `addPaths`+construct occurs using `resource.getPath()` (already-resolved) + welcomeFile \u2014 must also be re-validated for containment, not just the first resolution.\n- The `getServletPath()`-only source branch (no `RequestDispatcher.INCLUDE_*` attributes present) at AbstractResourceHandler.java:50, in addition to the `INCLUDE_SERVLET_PATH`/`INCLUDE_PATH_INFO` attribute-based branch (lines 46-48) \u2014 both must be covered since they converge on the same `addPaths`/`getResource(String)` sink.\n- Both concrete handler/resource pairs: `ExternalResourceHandler`/`ExternalResource` (filesystem) and `ClassPathResourceHandler`/`ClassPathResource` (classpath, when backed by an exploded directory rather than a jar) \u2014 a fix applied to only one class leaves the other exploitable via the identical technique.\n- Double-encoded or literal `%2e%2e` sequences reaching this code as literal characters (e.g. if a servlet container or reverse proxy does not decode before dispatch) should also be considered, though this PoV's primary demonstrated bypass is the unescaped backslash case, which needs no encoding at all.",
